<header class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Publishing handoff</p><h1>Take the article into your next tool.</h1><p class="lead">Choose a formatted clipboard copy, one article file, or a kit with the files and instructions together. Inspect the result in your own destination before publishing.</p></header>
<h2 id="wordpress">Take editable blocks into WordPress</h2><p>Paste plain text or simple Markdown in <a href="/write/#draft">the writing workspace</a>, read the preview, and review your claims and permissions. Choose WordPress block editor under Choose your export, then check the review approval box. Page metadata is optional.</p><ol><li>Open a new, empty draft post on your own WordPress site. Put the title in its separate title field. Copy title separately is available when a valid title is present.</li><li>Select Copy WordPress blocks. In the WordPress editor, open Options, select Code editor, and paste into the post body. Use the full post Code editor, not a Custom HTML block or the Classic Editor. Start with an empty post to avoid replacing existing writing.</li><li>Return to Visual editor. The supported content becomes editable paragraph, heading, flat list, quote, and code blocks. Check paragraph breaks, emphasis, links, and heading levels. Body H1 headings become H2, and numbered lists start at 1.</li><li>Add images, excerpt, slug, and any SEO description in the appropriate WordPress fields or plugin. These are not inserted into the body. Rebuild tables, nested lists, embeds, reference links, and other unsupported formatting in WordPress; their source stays literal text.</li><li>Preview the actual post, including its mobile view, before publishing yourself. Themes, plugins, and editor versions can change the result. Exporting does not verify factual claims or URLs.</li></ol><p>If clipboard access is unavailable, Download WordPress blocks provides an HTML file. Open it in a text editor to copy the complete source, including block comments, into the post Code editor. Opening the file in a browser shows a reading view, not its block source. Inspect or manually copy the approved blocks offers a selectable source field after review. Editing or changing destinations clears approval and that temporary output.</p><p>No WordPress credentials, API requests, image downloads, or website connection are involved. The title and page fields remain separate. Source notes and private review questions are excluded from the blocks. <a href="https://wordpress.org/documentation/article/wordpress-block-editor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official WordPress editor help (new tab)</a>.</p>
<h2 id="wordpress-kit">Keep the complete WordPress handoff together</h2><p>The workspace shows line-specific WordPress handoff findings while you edit. These combine limited writing-pattern checks with some format checks for literal images, source HTML, tables, nested lists, reference links, task-list markers, strike-through, separators, body H1 headings, and numbered-list starts. Select Inspect line to focus and select the current passage. Nothing changes automatically. Edits refresh findings; switching destinations clears their hidden list. The first 40 findings are shown, with a count when more exist. This is not an exhaustive Markdown parser, quality score, or verification of facts, permissions, grammar, or working URLs.</p><p>Under Post fields &amp; complete WordPress kit, use Edit optional post fields to open the existing excerpt, filename, and description fields. Editing clears approval. After reviewing again, copy each field separately. Excerpt goes in the post Excerpt setting; filename becomes a suggested Slug/permalink when available; description belongs in your SEO tool only if it has that field. An excerpt is optional and its display depends on the theme. Blocks do not require these fields.</p><p>A WordPress kit needs a valid title and article. Empty optional fields remain empty. If the filename is empty or invalid, the kit uses a safe suggestion from the title and discloses it; the editor field is unchanged. Review the six-file list, approve, and choose Download WordPress kit. Extract the ZIP locally before using it.</p><table class="format-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">How to use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">wordpress-blocks.html</th><td>Open in a text editor and copy all source, including block comments, into the full WordPress Code editor. Return to Visual editor and inspect.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">article-body.md</th><td>Keep the original Markdown body for further editing. It is not the block source.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">reading-page.html</th><td>Open in a browser to review the article. It does not predict every theme or plugin.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">page-details.json</th><td>Manually place title and optional excerpt, slug, and description in their appropriate fields. This is not a CMS import file.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">REVIEW.txt</th><td>Current line-specific handoff findings and a final publishing checklist. Regenerate the kit after article edits.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">README.txt</th><td>Follow the WordPress-specific steps and field mapping.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The kit excludes private source and review notes, revisions, and authentication. Its article and page fields can still contain private information you entered. It is unencrypted, bounded to 512 KiB, does not restore a workspace, and does not upload or publish. Save a separate private workspace backup for unfinished work. <a href="https://wordpress.org/documentation/article/page-post-settings-sidebar/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official WordPress field help (new tab)</a>.</p>
<h2>Paste into a website editor</h2><ol><li><a href="/write/">Finish your article in the workspace</a>, compare it with your source, and check the review box.</li><li>Select Copy formatted article. Basic headings, lists, emphasis, code, and supported web links are included. The default copy excludes the title, so you can enter it in your editor's separate title field.</li><li>If your destination needs a document title inside the body, select Include the title when copying formatted text and review again before copying.</li><li>Paste into an editor that accepts HTML clipboard content. The copy also includes a plain-text representation with readable link addresses.</li><li>Check the actual pasted headings, lists, paragraph breaks, title, links, and mobile preview before publishing.</li></ol><p>Browser and destination support varies. Some editors discard formatting or change it. This is a clipboard handoff, not a verified CMS integration. If formatted copying is unavailable or blocked, Copy article body supplies Markdown, Download reading page provides HTML, and the visible preview can be selected manually. Your writing stays in the workspace.</p>
<h2>Download a publishing kit</h2><p>Enter a valid title and article, approve the result, and expand Take a complete publishing kit. Inspect the file list and notices, then select Download publishing kit. The ZIP is prepared locally. Extract it into a local folder and open its reading page first.</p>
<table class="format-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">What to do with it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">article-body.md</th><td>Continue editing or paste into a destination that accepts Markdown. Title and page fields are excluded.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">reading-page.html</th><td>Open locally to read and inspect the article. Manually add it to static hosting only after reviewing its destination name and preview.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">page-details.json</th><td>Use its title, excerpt, description, and suggested safe filename for a manual handoff. Empty fields stay empty. This is not an article import or a universal CMS import file.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">page.md, when included</th><td>Use complete page Markdown in a repository that supports its metadata. The format choice determines whether the file contains a title heading.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">README.txt</th><td>Follow the file-specific next steps and destination review checklist.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Finish page details when needed</h2><p>A kit needs only the title and article. Complete the excerpt, description, and a valid filename to include page.md. Otherwise the preview and README list the fields to finish. For page.md, expand Markdown with page details and choose Website supplies the title heading or Include a title heading in the file.</p><p>If the filename is empty or invalid, the ZIP filename and page-details slug use a safe suggestion from the title. The original editor field is kept, and the preview explains this decision. The kit does not invent missing summaries, dates, layout settings, or facts.</p>
<h2>Choose one destination format</h2><p>Do not upload the whole kit as a website. For a Markdown repository, add page.md on a feature branch and adapt its metadata to the actual site. For static HTML, choose a destination name for reading-page.html in a directory that serves HTML. For another editor, use the clipboard or article body and enter page fields manually. <a href="/help/markdown-export/">Review the export guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Keep article files and private work separate</h2><p>The kit and clipboard copies exclude source notes, audience, purpose, article type, private review questions, check results, and sign-in fields. They can still contain private information you put in the article. Review that content before sharing or adding files to a public repository.</p><p>A publishing kit is unencrypted and cannot restore a complete workspace. Use an explicit <a href="/help/local-workspace/">private workspace backup</a> separately to keep source and review notes. Export preferences and temporary kit previews are excluded from those version-1 backups.</p>
<h2>Know what the handoff covers</h2><p>The reading renderer and formatted copy share the same limited Markdown subset. Source HTML, images, relative URLs, and unsupported links remain text. Supported absolute web links load only when a reader follows them. The ZIP uses ordinary stored text files without compression, under a 512 KiB bound. Files carry a fixed archive timestamp, not an edit-history timestamp.</p><p>Exporting does not upload, connect to a repository, approve claims, or publish. The draft flag is metadata and works only when your destination honors it. Check the actual saved files, rendering, links, permissions, and factual claims in the destination.</p><p><a class="button" href="/write/#exports">Open review &amp; export</a> · <a href="/guides/review/">Use the review checklist</a></p>
